  "explain": {
   "text": "Cut every piece of a bar in two and you have twice as many pieces, so the same amount takes twice as many of them.[^1] That is why 3/4 and 6/8 shade exactly the same amount. They are equivalent fractions: different names for one amount.\n\nSo you can multiply the top and the bottom by the same number, or divide both by the same number, and the fraction keeps its value.[^2] Any number but 0 will do: you can't divide by 0, and multiplying by 0 gives 0/0, which names no amount at all. Dividing down as far as it goes is called simplifying, so 6/8 simplifies to 3/4. Equivalent fractions are the same number, so they sit at the same point on the number line.[^3]\n\n**Multiply or divide the top and bottom by the same number, and the fraction keeps its size.**"
  },
  "analogy": null,
  "practice": {
   "text": "**Rule of thumb:** To give a fraction a new name of the same size, whatever you do to the bottom, do to the top. Multiply or divide by any number but 0, never add or take away.\n\n**Worked example:** A 3/8 inch drill bit, laid against a rule marked in sixteenths. An eighth is two sixteenths, so multiply the top and the bottom by 2: the bit spans 6/16 inch, six sixteenth-steps from 0. Drill gauges mark that size as 3/8, its simplest name.\n\n**Common mistakes**\n- Adding the same number to the top and bottom. 3/4 is not the same as 4/5.\n- Simplifying by taking away. 6/9 simplifies to 2/3, not 3/6."
  },
